WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.180
An osprey lifts a menhaden from the Chesapeake Bay.

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.720
Where did the energy inside that fish begin?

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.160
Follow one glowing packet backward through the moving water.

4
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:19.387
The Bay is an ecosystem, which includes living things and their nonliving

5
00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:24.487
surroundings. Light, water, and temperature are abiotic factors.

6
00:00:26.087 --> 00:00:29.387
Organisms, their parts, and their wastes are biotic factors.

7
00:00:30.647 --> 00:00:33.527
A change in either kind can reshape the community.

8
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:39.220
The trail ends at sunlight reaching phytoplankton, tiny drifting algae.

9
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.920
They are producers because they make food using light energy.

10
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.160
That stored energy can enter the living food web.

11
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.000
Most ecosystems need new energy entering again and again.

12
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.090
A zooplankton eats the producer.

13
00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.290
A menhaden eats that zooplankton.

14
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.390
An osprey catches the menhaden.

15
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:06.170
Each eater receives some stored energy from its food.

16
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:10.810
The packet changes location, but it still follows the food.

17
00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.210
The arrows are missing.

18
00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.950
If each arrow shows energy movement, should it point toward the food or toward the

19
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:21.990
eater? Choose before anything moves.

20
00:01:27.983 --> 00:01:32.643
Arrows point from food to eater, the same direction the energy packet moves.

21
00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:35.703
This straight route is one food chain.

22
00:01:37.043 --> 00:01:39.163
It is one path, not the whole ecosystem.

23
00:01:40.743 --> 00:01:43.683
Read every arrow by asking where the stored energy goes.

24
00:01:44.817 --> 00:01:47.597
Menhaden can also eat phytoplankton directly.

25
00:01:49.217 --> 00:01:50.857
Larger fish can eat menhaden too.

26
00:01:52.117 --> 00:01:53.717
New arrows join the first path.

27
00:01:55.117 --> 00:01:56.957
Linked food chains become a food web.

28
00:01:58.277 --> 00:02:01.437
That web reveals how a change can reach several organisms.

29
00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:06.057
On the longer path, the osprey is a third-order consumer.

30
00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:09.337
On a shorter path, it is second-order.

31
00:02:10.677 --> 00:02:12.137
The animal stays the same.

32
00:02:13.437 --> 00:02:15.477
Its order depends on the chosen path.

33
00:02:16.857 --> 00:02:19.637
Consumer order describes a position on one route.

34
00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:24.327
Dead matter and wastes carry stored energy from every level.

35
00:02:25.827 --> 00:02:30.367
Dashed routes lead downward to decomposers, including many bacteria and fungi.

36
00:02:31.767 --> 00:02:35.027
Their work returns matter, but energy keeps moving onward.

37
00:02:36.287 --> 00:02:40.407
The decomposer branch connects to every feeding level, not just the top.

38
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:43.150
Now watch each feeding step.

39
00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.230
Much energy follows other routes, so the packet continuing upward becomes smaller.

40
00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:54.690
Energy has not disappeared; different paths now hold it or release heat.

41
00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.710
At each feeding step, less energy is available to the next level.

42
00:03:00.783 --> 00:03:04.643
The osprey's energy began with sunlight captured by producers.

43
00:03:06.103 --> 00:03:08.223
A food web shows many connected routes.

44
00:03:09.443 --> 00:03:14.223
The shrinking packet explains why higher feeding levels have less energy to share.

45
00:03:15.443 --> 00:03:19.083
The web maps routes, while the packet maps what remains available.
